INSERT INTO `submissions` (`id`, `manuscript_id`, `name`, `email`, `phone`, `institution`, `journal`, `title`, `cover_letter`, `abstract`, `disclosures`, `manuscript_file`, `figures_file`, `tables_file`, `status`, `created_at`) VALUES
(22, '761376745', 'Oluba Olarewaju', 'olubamichael2023@gmail.com', '07030496639', 'David Umahi Fed University of Health Sciences', 'NISEB_JOURNAL', 'LETTER OF APPOINTMENT AS EDITOR-IN-CHIEF', 'This manuscript is being submitted to text run the submission process of this journal.', 'The National Executive Committee of the Society for Experimental Biology of Nigeria (NISEB) has resolved to appoint you as the Editor-in-Chief of the Bioscience Research Journal at its duly convened meeting. This decision reflects your scholarly distinction, professional integrity, and leadership capacity in advancing the publishing vision of the Society.', 'None', 'uploads/manuscript_68e7f5e87551e.docx', NULL, NULL, 'Pending Fee', '2025-10-09 17:50:32'),
(23, '604693147', 'Omowumi Kayode', 'otkayode@mtu.edu.ng', '08037802662', 'mountain top university', 'NISEB_JOURNAL', 'Role of Nutrients In Immuno-Therapeutic Control of Programmed Cell Death: A Consise Review', 'COVER LETTER\r\n26/09/202\r\nNISEB Journal\r\n\r\nDear Editor,\r\nI am pleased to submit our manuscript entitled â€œNutritional-Immunological Crosstalk and Programmed Cell Death: Implications for Immunotherapyâ€ for consideration for publication in NISEB Journal. This review explores the intricate interactions between nutrients and programmed cell death pathways, emphasizing their potential as modulators of immune responses and their implications for immunotherapeutic interventions.\r\nKey Highlights of the Study:\r\nâ€¢	Metabolic Influence: The availability of nutrients, particularly glucose and amino acids, significantly affects the metabolic state of immune cells, impacting their ability to execute apoptosis and the efficacy of immunotherapies.\r\nâ€¢	Antioxidant Role: Antioxidants can modulate programmed cell death by reducing reactive oxygen species (ROS) in cancer cells, influencing the sensitivity of these cells to ROS-mediated therapies.\r\nâ€¢	Essential Nutrients: Vitamins D and zinc are crucial for optimal immune cell function, and their adequate intake can enhance immune cell performance in apoptosis and overall immunotherapeutic outcomes.\r\nâ€¢	Gut Microbiome: The balance of the gut microbiome affects nutrient metabolism and immune responses, indirectly influencing programmed cell death during immunotherapy.\r\nOur findings underscore the potential of nutrient-based interventions to fine-tune cell death mechanisms, offering a more precise and personalized approach to immune modulation. This review provides a comprehensive understanding of the role of nutrients in immuno-therapeutic control of programmed cell death, which could revolutionize immunotherapy strategies and improve patient outcomes.\r\nWe believe that our manuscript will be of great interest to the readers of NISEB Journal due to the growing interest in the intersection of nutrition and immunotherapy. The manuscript has not been published elsewhere and is not under consideration by any other journal.\r\nWe have adhered to the ethical guidelines for research and publication, and all authors have approved the manuscript and agree with its submission to NISEB Journal. Thank you for considering our manuscript for publication. We look forward to your positive response.\r\n\r\nSincerely,\r\nOmowumi T. Kayode\r\nNutritional and Reproductive Biochemistry Laboratory\r\nDepartment of Biochemistry\r\nCollege of Basic and Applied Sciences\r\nMountain Top University, Prayer City, Nigeria\r\nEmail: otkayode@mtu.edu.ng\r\nTel.: +234 803 780 2662', 'Abstract Programmed cell death is a vital physiological process that maintains homeostasis and regulates immune responses. Recent advances in immunotherapy have revealed the significant role nutrients play in modulating these pathways. This review explores the emerging intersection of nutrition and immunology, emphasizing how vitamins, minerals, and dietary components influence programmed cell death, particularly apoptosis, in immune cells. Nutrient imbalancesâ€”whether deficiencies or excessesâ€”can disrupt the equilibrium between pro-survival and pro-apoptotic signals, thereby affecting immune function. Conversely, nutrient-rich diets enhance immune cell vitality and resilience against infections and malignancies.\r\n\r\nBeyond apoptosis, nutrients are increasingly recognized for their roles in regulating necroptosis and pyroptosisâ€”cell death modalities once considered purely inflammatory. These pathways are now viewed as potential therapeutic targets in immunological disorders. Nutrient-based interventions may offer precise control over these mechanisms, helping to mitigate immune dysfunction or overactivity.\r\n\r\nThe review also highlights the promise of nutrient-driven immunotherapies in treating cancer, autoimmune diseases, and infections. By harnessing the modulatory effects of nutrients on programmed cell death, immunotherapy strategies can become more targeted and personalized. A deeper understanding of these interactions may pave the way for innovative treatments and improved patient outcomes.\r\n\r\nKeywords Immunotherapy; Immunotherapeutic control; Nutrients; Programmed cell death', 'NONE', 'uploads/manuscript_68ed7670835a3.docx', NULL, NULL, 'Pending Fee', '2025-10-13 22:00:16'),
(24, '399338862', 'Omowumi Kayode', 'wumikayot2024@gmail.com', '08037802662', 'mountain top university', 'NISEB_JOURNAL', 'xxx', 'qwertyuiop;\'mncxzxcvbnm,./.,mnbv', 'asdfghjkl;,mnbvcxzxcvbnm,lkjhgfdsaqwertyuio', '', 'uploads/manuscript_6917b5067527c.docx', NULL, NULL, 'Pending Fee', '2025-11-14 23:02:30');
